The conditional comparison arguments apply with all original initializations retained in the conditioning fields. In particular, condition on $\mathcal{W}^{(0)}$, the action labels, and the noises in an arm. The rotated directions and parameters are then fixed, and that arm’s context rows are still independent standard Gaussians. The bounds in 1.1.1/1.1.11/1.2.36 and 1.1.1/1.1.10/1.3.110 are uniform in these fixed directions and initializations once their coordinate bounds and envelope are specified. Their error bounds are polynomial in $\varkappa_{\ast,a}^{\prime}\log d$ times a negative power of $d$, so they vanish in probability under (1.1.1/1.1.9/1.1.86). Conditional probability bounds may be truncated at one before averaging over the initialization and noise. The empirical noise moments are unchanged by rotation. Conditioning additionally on all initializations also preserves the independence used when predictions are evaluated on another arm’s observations. Thus, the risk and value consistency proofs apply to the rotated system, as does the finite-grid selection argument.


