The identity also holds for signed $b$ when $\int|b(q,\Theta_{a})|\,dP_{Q}(q)<\infty$ almost surely, with conditional expectations interpreted by this finite fresh-draw integral.@equation
\mathbb{E}[b(Q,\Theta_{a})\,|\,\mathcal{H}]=\int b(q,\Theta_{a})\,dP_{Q}(q)\qquad\text{almost surely}.@proof
Proof.
The variable $\Theta_{a}$ is $\mathcal{H}$-measurable, and the conditional law of $Q$ given $\mathcal{H}$ is its marginal law. The identity follows first for bounded $b$ and then for nonnegative $b$ by monotone convergence. For signed $b$, apply the nonnegative identity to its positive and negative parts; the assumed absolute-integral bound makes both terms finite.
