1.1.1/1.1.10/1.3.127


The first $q$ coordinate maps have the polynomial local-Lipschitz bounds established in 1.1.1/1.1.10/1.3.41; their input-cutoff versions have global Lipschitz constants and values at zero bounded by a fixed polynomial in the cutoff radius and $\varkappa_{\ast,a}$. Thus, the hypotheses used by the averaged GFOM theorem and the polynomial-growth delocalization proposition are exactly those verified in that lemma.


For every history argument and every time index $j$, the relevant block Jacobians have the form


1.1.1/1.1.10/1.3.128


Indeed, the first $q$ output coordinates do not depend on the policy-index histories, and the policy-index outputs are constant or zero. The Onsager blocks involving the first $q$ coordinates, the coefficients $\rho_{t,s}$, and the deterministic shifts $D_{t}$ are therefore unchanged from the original HI25 recursion. All Jacobian blocks involving the appended coordinates are zero. Substitution into the generic asymmetric GFOM recursion in HI25 Definition therefore gives the same first-$q$-coordinate state evolution, with the appended covariance computed next.


We now compute the augmented row covariance generated by that recursion. For $s\geq 1$, let the column-side state entering the $s$th row covariance be


1.1.1/1.1.10/1.3.129


with the initialization $Q_{j}^{(0)}=(D_{-1})_{j\cdot}$. The centered column states satisfy $\mathbb{E}_{a}^{(0)}\mathfrak{V}_{j}^{(r)}=0$, so $\mathbb{E}_{a}^{(0)}Q_{j}^{(s)}=(D_{s-1})_{j\cdot}$. The column-side vector in the augmented recursion is $(Q_{j}^{(s)},\sqrt{n}\,\Upsilon_{j\cdot})$. Hence, the cross-time covariance block for augmented row states at times $s$ and $r$ is


