\displaystyle\left|\vartheta_{k}(\widetilde{u}_{k}^{([0:t])})-\vartheta_{k}(\widetilde{\check{u}}_{k}^{([0:t])})\right|\leq\Lambda_{\vartheta}\left(1+\|u_{k}^{([0:t])}\|+\|\check{u}_{k}^{([0:t])}\|+2\sqrt{t+1}\|X_{k\cdot}\Upsilon\|\right)^{\mathfrak{p}-1}\|u_{k}^{([0:t])}-\check{u}_{k}^{([0:t])}\|.@equation
\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{a}^{(0)}\left[\left|\frac{1}{N_{a}}\sum_{k=1}^{N_{a}}\vartheta_{k}(\widetilde{u}_{k}^{([0:t])})-\frac{1}{N_{a}}\sum_{k=1}^{N_{a}}\mathbb{E}_{a}^{(0)}\vartheta_{k}\left(\left\{\widetilde{\Theta}_{k}^{(s)}(\widetilde{\mathfrak{U}}^{([0:s])})\right\}_{s=0}^{t}\right)\right|^{\mathfrak{r}}\right]\leq(C_{t}\Lambda_{\vartheta}\varkappa_{\ast,a})^{c_{t}}d^{-1/c_{t}}.@equation
\displaystyle\mathbb{P}_{a}^{(0)}\left(\|u^{(t)}\|_{\infty}\vee\|v^{(t)}\|_{\infty}>B_{t}\right)@equation
\displaystyle\quad\leq(N_{a}+n_{L,q})\max_{k,j}\mathbb{P}_{a}^{(0)}\left(\|u_{k}^{(t)}\|_{\infty}\vee\|v_{j}^{(t)}\|_{\infty}>B_{t}\right)\leq d^{-101},@equation
(\widetilde{u}^{(t)})^{\mathrm{idx}}=X\Upsilon,\qquad(\widetilde{v}^{(t)})^{\mathrm{idx}}=0.@equation
\mathbb{P}_{a}^{(0)}\bigl(\|X\Upsilon\|_{\infty}>z\bigr)\leq 2N_{a}\ell e^{-z^{2}/2}.The appended coordinate vector is the observed index $X_{a;k\cdot}\Upsilon\in\mathbb{R}^{\ell}$, and the proposition identifies its joint state evolution with the auxiliary iterates, including their cross-covariances.# Appendix E Weighted Risk Estimation with the Empirical Noise DistributionWe first compare the corrected empirical risk with a risk evaluated at an independent Gaussian context and a noise value sampled uniformly from the realized arm-wise noise values. The index $\pi$ in $\mathcal{E}_{a,\pi}^{(t)}$ selects this noise value. This intermediate risk is used only in the proof; the procedure does not require the unobserved noise values. The next section replaces the sampled noise value by an independent draw from the arm-wise noise distribution.## E.1 Auxiliary states and comparison estimatesFor the next comparisons, fix an arm $a$ and an iteration $t$. Let $\mathcal{I}_{a}=\{i_{a,1}<\cdots<i_{a,N_{a}}\}$ and relabel the arm-wise sample by@equation
X_{k}\coloneqq X_{i_{a,k}},\qquad R_{k}\coloneqq R_{i_{a,k}},\qquad\xi_{k}\coloneqq R_{i_{a,k}}-\varphi_{a}^{\ast}(\beta_{a}^{\top}X_{i_{a,k}}),\qquad k\in[N_{a}].Let $X\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{a}\times d}$ denote the arm-wise design matrix with $k$-th row $X_{k}^{\top}$. Set $N\coloneqq N_{a}$, $\phi_{a}\coloneqq N/d$, $\beta\coloneqq\beta_{a}$, and $h\coloneqq h_{a}$. Define@equation
\beta_{[q]}\coloneqq\bigl(\beta\,|\,0_{d\times(q-1)}\bigr)\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times q}.Conditional on $\mathcal{G}_{a}$, which determines $N$, let $\pi_{N}$ be uniform on $[N]$ and independent of the arm-wise design, the fresh Gaussian vectors, and the state-evolution Gaussian variables. It remains uniform and independent of the fresh variables after conditioning on $\widehat{\mathcal{F}}_{a}$. Under this relabeling, the envelope $\varkappa_{\ast,a}$ in (1.1.1/1.1.9/1.1.46) is exactly HI25’s envelope in (). Hence, by (1.1.1/1.1.9/1.1.50), for every fixed $C,c>0$,@equation
(\varkappa_{\ast,a}\log d)^{C}d^{-c}=o_{p}(1).We use this repeatedly below to turn the polynomial error bounds at each fixed iteration into $o_{p}(1)$ remainders. Throughout, we work on $E_{\mathrm{cnt}}$ and condition on $\mathcal{G}_{a}$, using the expectation $\mathbb{E}_{a}^{(0)}$ and probability $\mathbb{P}_{a}^{(0)}$ from 1.1.1/1.1.10/1.2.9. The covariates $\{X_{k}\}_{k\in[N]}$ remain random and form the Gaussian HI25 design, while $\beta,\Upsilon,W_{a}^{(0)}$, and $\xi_{[N]}$ are fixed. Empirical quantities are measurable with respect to $\widehat{\mathcal{F}}_{a}$ from the same proposition, whereas the state-evolution comparisons are conditional on $\mathcal{G}_{a}$.Let@equation
\hat{u}^{(0)}\coloneqq X\beta_{[q]},\qquad\hat{u}^{(s)}\coloneqq XW_{1}^{(s-1)}\quad(s\geq 1),let $U^{(t)}$ be the theoretical corrected hidden-state matrix defined below, let $u^{(s)}$ be the original $q$-dimensional auxiliary GFOM iterate from Proposition 1.1.1/1.1.10/1.3.110, and let $V^{(s)}=(V_{\alpha}^{(s)})_{\alpha\in[2:L]}$ and $\rho_{t,s}$ be the arm-wise state-evolution objects. For each row $k$, write $R_{k;[q]}\coloneqq(R_{k},0,\ldots,0)^{\top}$ and $\xi_{k;[q]}\coloneqq(\xi_{k},0,\ldots,0)^{\top}$. Let@equation
R_{[q]}\coloneqq\begin{pmatrix}R_{1;[q]}^{\top}\\
\vdots\\
R_{N;[q]}^{\top}\end{pmatrix},\qquad\xi_{[q]}\coloneqq\begin{pmatrix}\xi_{1;[q]}^{\top}\\
\vdots\\
\xi_{N;[q]}^{\top}\end{pmatrix}.Recall the padded reward link in (1.1.1/1.1.9/1.1.58). For a matrix $W\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times q}$, let@equation
\varphi_{a,[q]}^{\ast}(W)\coloneqq\begin{pmatrix}\varphi_{a,[q]}^{\ast}(W_{1\cdot})^{\top}\\
\vdots\\
\varphi_{a,[q]}^{\ast}(W_{N\cdot})^{\top}\end{pmatrix}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times q},that is, the same padded link applied rowwise. Define the matrix-valued arm-wise residual map@equation
\mathscr{R}^{\mathrm{arm}}_{B}(U,W)\coloneqq\mathscr{G}_{B}(U)-\varphi_{a,[q]}^{\ast}(W)-\xi_{[q]}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times q},and, for each row $k$, its rowwise version@equation
\mathscr{R}^{\mathrm{arm}}_{B;k}(u,w)\coloneqq\mathscr{G}_{B;k}(u)-\varphi_{a,[q]}^{\ast}(w)-\xi_{k;[q]}\in\mathbb{R}^{q}.When a row index is attached to $\mathscr{G}$, it denotes the rowwise application of the same feed-forward map; the map $\mathscr{G}_{B}$ itself has no additional dependence on $k$. Here $B=(B_{2},\ldots,B_{L})\in(\mathbb{R}^{q\times q})^{[2:L]}$ denotes the deeper-layer parameter tuple. Define the derivative of the row loss with respect to its hidden state by@equation
\mathfrak{S}_{k}(u,w,B)\coloneqq\left\{\nabla_{u}\frac{1}{2}\|\mathscr{R}^{\mathrm{arm}}_{B;k}(u,w)\|^{2}\right\}^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{1\times q},and let $\mathfrak{S}(U,W,B)$ collect these rows. This is the HI25 pre-gradient map with the padded reward link $\varphi_{a,[q]}^{\ast}$. Along the gradient-descent trajectory,@equation
\mathfrak{S}_{k}(\hat{u}_{k}^{(s)},\hat{u}_{k}^{(0)},W_{[2:L]}^{(s-1)})=\Psi_{i_{a,k},a}^{(s-1)}.The deeper-layer derivatives use the same padded residual. All HI25 comparisons invoked below therefore use the extension in 1.1.1/1.1.9/1.1.66. By the zero-column invariance established in 1.1.1/1.1.9/1.1.1, the padded residual is supported on its first coordinate along the gradient-descent, auxiliary, and state-evolution trajectories. Its squared norm is therefore exactly the scalar reward residual squared. Then, since $\hat{u}_{k}^{(0)}=(\beta^{\top}X_{k},0,\ldots,0)$,@equation
R_{k;[q]}=\varphi_{a,[q]}^{\ast}(\hat{u}_{k}^{(0)})+\xi_{k;[q]}.For the comparisons below, write@equation
\widehat{U}^{(t)}\coloneqq\widehat{U}_{a}^{(t)},\qquad W^{(t)}\coloneqq W_{a}^{(t)},\qquad\hat{v}_{1}^{(t)}\coloneqq\sqrt{d}\,W_{1}^{(t)},\qquad v_{1}^{(t)}\coloneqq v_{1,a}^{(t)}.The auxiliary GFOM recursion makes $v_{1}^{(t)}$ a measurable function of the arm-wise design, response, initialization, and fixed problem parameters. Thus, $v_{1}^{(t)}$ is $\widehat{\mathcal{F}}_{a}$-measurable. Set@equation
D_{-1}\coloneqq(\sqrt{d}\,\beta,0_{d\times(q-1)}).For $s\geq 0$, write $D_{s}\coloneqq D_{a,s}$ for the arm-wise deterministic state-evolution shifts. To compare $\widehat{U}^{(t)}$ with the auxiliary GFOM, we first replace the estimated Onsager coefficients by $\rho_{t,s}$, then replace the deeper weights in the loss gradients by $V^{(s-1)}$, and finally replace the GD hidden states by the auxiliary iterates. These three replacements define, respectively,@equation
\displaystyle U^{(t)}@equation
\displaystyle\bar{U}^{(t)}@equation
\displaystyle\bar{U}_{\mathrm{aux}}^{(t)}Let $Z\sim N(0,I_{d})$ be independent of $\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{tr}}$, and define@equation
G_{t}\coloneqq\left(d^{-1/2}v_{1}^{(t)\top}Z,\,d^{-1/2}D_{-1}^{\top}Z,\,\Upsilon^{\top}Z\right),\qquad G_{0,t}\coloneqq\left(\mathfrak{U}_{a}^{(t+1)},\,\mathfrak{U}_{a}^{(0)},\,\mathfrak{U}_{\Upsilon,a}\right).Let $\widetilde{\Sigma}_{t}$ be the covariance of $G_{t}$ over the fresh draw $Z$, holding $(\mathcal{G}_{a},v_{1}^{(t)})$ fixed, and let $\widetilde{\Sigma}_{0,t}$ be the conditional covariance of $G_{0,t}$ under $\mathbb{E}_{a}^{(0)}$.@lemma
