\displaystyle\leq\min_{\mathbf{t}\in\mathcal{T}^{K}}\mathcal{R}_{\nu}^{\circ}(g_{\mathbf{t}})+o_{p}(1),## G.4 Suboptimal stopping after full-data refittingWith a fixed validation fraction, cross-validation estimates the test risk of models trained at a different limiting sample-to-dimension ratio. The following example shows that ten-fold cross-validation can correctly rank these models yet select a suboptimal iterate after full-data refitting.Let $F_{1},\ldots,F_{10}$ partition $[m]$ independently of the observations, with $|F_{j}|=m/10+o(m)$. Write $g_{a,t}^{(-j)}$ for the arm-$a$ model trained on $\mathcal{I}_{a}\setminus F_{j}$, using the same initialization and learning rates as the full-data model $g_{a,t}$. On $\mathcal{T}=\{0,1\}$, define@equation
\widehat{\mathcal{E}}^{\rm CV}_{a}(t)\coloneqq\frac{1}{N_{a}}\sum_{j=1}^{10}\sum_{i\in\mathcal{I}_{a}\cap F_{j}}\{R_{i}-g_{a,t}^{(-j)}(X_{i})\}^{2},\qquad\hat{t}_{a}^{\rm CV}\in\arg\min_{t\in\mathcal{T}}\widehat{\mathcal{E}}^{\rm CV}_{a}(t).The importance weights equal one in the example below. Let $t_{a}^{\star}\in\arg\min_{t\in\mathcal{T}}\mathcal{R}_{\nu,a}^{\circ}(g_{a,t})$ be the full-data risk minimizer, and set $\hat{\mathbf{t}}^{\rm CV}=(\hat{t}_{1}^{\rm CV},\ldots,\hat{t}_{K}^{\rm CV})$. Break ties toward $t=0$ and use any measurable convention when a required arm count is zero; the latter event has probability tending to zero.@theorem
kind: proposition (Proposition G.2 (Suboptimal stopping after cross-validation).)
Let $X_{i}\overset{\rm iid}{\sim}N(0,I_{d})$, let $K\geq 2$ be fixed, and assume context-independent logging with $\mu(a)=\nu(a\mid x)=1/K$ and $m/d\to K$. Take noiseless rewards $R_{i}=g^{\ast}(X_{i},A_{i})=\beta^{\top}X_{i}$, where $\beta=d^{-1/2}(1,\ldots,1)^{\top}$. For every full-data and fold-complement fit, use the depth-two, width-one network with identity activation, initialization $W_{a,1}^{(0)}=0$, $W_{a,2}^{(0)}=1$, and first-layer step size $\eta=39/40$. The second-layer step size may be any fixed positive number satisfying Assumption 1.1.1/1.1.9/1.1.35. Then, as $d\to\infty$,


