The first two brackets are uniformly $o_{p}(1)$ by 1.1.1/1.1.4/1.3.15. Rearranging yields the uniform claim. The selected-model statement follows by setting $\mathbf{t}=\hat{\mathbf{t}}$, since $\hat{\mathbf{t}}\in\mathcal{T}^{K}$.
∎@equation
\widehat{\mathcal{V}}_{\mathrm{ADR}}(\mathbf{t})-\widehat{\mathcal{V}}_{\mathrm{ADM}}(\mathbf{t})=\left[\widehat{\mathcal{V}}_{\mathrm{ADR}}(\mathbf{t})-\mathcal{V}(\nu)\right]-\left[\widehat{\mathcal{V}}_{\mathrm{ADM}}(\mathbf{t})-\mathcal{V}_{g_{\mathbf{t}}}(\nu)\right]-\left[\mathcal{V}_{g_{\mathbf{t}}}(\nu)-\mathcal{V}(\nu)\right].## H.1 Error of uncorrected same-sample DRWe first recall the empirical-process condition used in classical analyses of DR estimators. We then examine whether this condition holds over the neural-network class considered here and derive the estimation error along the fitted gradient-descent trajectory. Let $P_{m,d}$ be the empirical measure of the training sample. For a function that depends on the fitted model, $P_{d}$ denotes expectation over an independent observation $Q=(X,A,R)$, conditional on $\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{tr}}$. Write $\mathbb{G}_{m,d}=\sqrt{m}(P_{m,d}-P_{d})$. Consider the DR estimator obtained by substituting the fitted networks $g_{\mathbf{t}}$ into $\psi_{g}$ and averaging over their training sample, without the Onsager correction:@equation
\widehat{\mathcal{V}}_{\mathrm{DR}}^{\mathrm{nv}}(\mathbf{t})\coloneqq\frac{1}{m}\sum_{i=1}^{m}\psi_{g_{\mathbf{t}}}(Q_{i}).Define $H_{g,d}(Q)\coloneqq\psi_{g}(Q)-\psi_{g^{\ast}}(Q)$. Known context-independent logging gives $P_{d}H_{g,d}=0$ for every realized integrable $\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{tr}}$-measurable $g$. Consequently, the exact decomposition@equation
\sqrt{m}\{\widehat{\mathcal{V}}_{\mathrm{DR}}^{\mathrm{nv}}(\mathbf{t})-\mathcal{V}(\nu)\}=\mathbb{G}_{m,d}\{\psi_{g^{\ast}}-\mathcal{V}(\nu)\}+\mathbb{G}_{m,d}H_{g_{\mathbf{t}},d}holds for the training-measurable fitted model $g_{\mathbf{t}}$.@theorem
kind: remark (Remark H.3 (Donsker conditions and cross-fitting).)
When the data distribution $P$ is fixed, asymptotic stochastic equicontinuity yields $\mathbb{G}_{m}H_{\widehat{g}}=o_{p}(1)$ if, with probability tending to one, $H_{\widehat{g}}$ belongs to a fixed $P$-Donsker class and $\|H_{\widehat{g}}\|_{P,2}=o_{p}(1)$. Cross-fitting evaluates each fitted reward model on observations excluded from its training sample. For each fold, conditional on its fitted model, the evaluation observations are independent with law $P$. With a fixed number of folds, conditional Chebyshev’s inequality therefore makes the cross-fitted empirical-process term $o_{p}(1)$ when each fitted score error converges to zero in $L^{2}(P)$, without a Donsker condition [1.1.1/2.1.1/1.1.12]. A fixed iterate grid gives finitely many models for each realized training sample. It does not put those models in a fixed Donsker class: as the data vary, each iterate ranges over a class whose input dimension grows with $d$.The next proposition yields a sequence of classes whose functions converge uniformly to zero in $L^{2}(P_{d})$, but whose empirical-process supremum does not converge to zero. Thus, local stochastic equicontinuity fails over the full class of permitted predictors. This does not determine the error of the particular predictors produced by gradient descent, which we analyze separately below.Consider the admissible submodel $g^{\ast}=0$ and $\nu=\mu$. For a fixed arm $a\in[K]$, define@equation
\displaystyle g_{\theta,d}(x,b)@equation
\displaystyle H_{\theta,d}(Q)and let $\mathcal{H}_{d}\coloneqq\{H_{\theta,d}:\|\theta\|_{2}\leq d^{-1/2}\}$.@theorem
