D_{a,d}(t)\coloneqq M_{a,d}^{\mathrm{tr},(t)}-M_{a}^{(t)},\qquad\mathfrak{b}_{d}(\mathbf{t})\coloneqq-\sum_{a=1}^{K}\mu(a)\{1-\mu(a)\}D_{a,d}(t_{a}).Here@equation
M_{a}^{(t)}=\mathbb{E}\left[w_{a}(Z)f_{W_{a}^{(t)}}(Z)\,|\,\mathcal{D}_{m},W_{a}^{(0)}\right]is the expected weighted prediction at an independent context, conditional on the fitted model. Thus, $D_{a,d}(t)$ is the difference between the state-evolution approximation to the training-sample average and the corresponding conditional expectation at an independent context. Both quantities can depend on $d$ and the training data; neither is assumed to have a limit. The weighted sum $\mathfrak{b}_{d}(\mathbf{t})$ will describe the leading estimation error of DR on the training sample.@lemma
