0\leq\mathcal{J}_{\mathrm{DR}}(g_{\hat{\mathbf{t}}})-\min_{\mathbf{t}\in\mathcal{T}^{K}}\mathcal{J}_{\mathrm{DR}}(g_{\mathbf{t}})\leq 2\sum_{a=1}^{K}(1-\mu(a))\Delta_{a}=o_{p}(1),@proof
Proof of 1.1.1/1.1.14/1.3.11.
It suffices to prove the conditional statement. The deterministic case is the same argument with a trivial $\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{tr}}$. Let $(X,A,R)$ be an independent test draw and write $\Delta_{a}(X)\coloneqq g^{\ast}(X,a)-g(X,a)$, $\nu_{a}\coloneqq\nu(a\,|\,X)$, and $\mu_{a}\coloneqq\mu(a)$. Because $R=g^{\ast}(X,A)+\varepsilon$ and $\mathbb{E}[\varepsilon\,|\,X,A,\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{tr}}]=0$,


$$\displaystyle\mathbb{E}\left[\psi_{g}(X,A,R)\,|\,X,\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{tr}}\right]$$


and the centered score is


$$\displaystyle\psi_{g}(X,A,R)-\mathbb{E}\left[\psi_{g}(X,A,R)\,|\,X,\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{tr}}\right]$$


Denote the second term on the right by $Q_{A}(X)$. The two terms are conditionally orthogonal because


$$\displaystyle\mathbb{E}\left[\frac{\nu(A\,|\,X)}{\mu(A)}\varepsilon Q_{A}(X)\,|\,X,\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{tr}}\right]=\sum_{a=1}^{K}\mu_{a}\frac{\nu_{a}}{\mu_{a}}Q_{a}(X)\mathbb{E}[\varepsilon\,|\,X,A=a,\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{tr}}]=0.$$


Moreover,


$$\displaystyle\mathbb{E}\left[\left\{\frac{\nu(A\,|\,X)}{\mu(A)}\varepsilon\right\}^{2}\,|\,X,\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{tr}}\right]$$


Therefore,


$$\displaystyle\operatorname{Var}\left(\psi_{g}(X,A,R)\,|\,X,\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{tr}}\right)$$


The model-dependent term is


$$\displaystyle\operatorname{Var}_{A\sim\mu(\cdot)}\left(\frac{\nu(A\,|\,X)}{\mu(A)}\Delta_{A}(X)\,|\,X,\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{tr}}\right)$$


Applying the law of total variance conditionally on $\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{tr}}$ yields


$$\displaystyle\operatorname{Var}\left(\psi_{g}(X,A,R)\,|\,\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{tr}}\right)$$


The first two terms are constant in $g$. Setting $g=g^{\ast}$ identifies their sum with $\operatorname{Var}(\psi_{g^{\ast}}\,|\,\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{tr}})$, which equals $V_{\mathrm{intrinsic}}$ because the test draw is independent of $\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{tr}}$. In addition,


$$\displaystyle\psi_{g}(X,A,R)-\psi_{g^{\ast}}(X,A,R)$$


This proves the third variance identity.


It remains to bound the model-dependent term. Conditionally on $(X,\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{tr}})$, let $B$ have law $\nu(\cdot\,|\,X)$ independently of $A$, and set


