Then


$$\displaystyle\mathbb{E}\left[Y\,|\,A,X,\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{tr}}\right]$$


so


1.1.1/1.1.14/1.3.37


On the other hand, conditional on $B=b$,


$$\displaystyle\mathbb{E}[Y\,|\,B=b,X,\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{tr}}]$$


Therefore


$$\displaystyle\operatorname{Var}\left(Y\,|\,X,\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{tr}}\right)$$


Finally, applying the law of total variance conditionally on $(X,\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{tr}})$ gives


$$\displaystyle\operatorname{Var}(Y\,|\,X,\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{tr}})$$


Averaging over $X$ proves $0\leq\mathcal{J}_{\mathrm{var}}(g)\leq\mathcal{J}_{\mathrm{DR}}(g)$.


Because $K\geq 2$ and $\mu(a)>0$ for every arm, each $\mu(a)<1$. Hence, $c_{-}(\mu)>0$. For each fixed arm $a$,


1.1.1/1.1.14/1.3.45


Multiplying by $\{g(X,a)-g^{\ast}(X,a)\}^{2}$, summing over $a$, and taking conditional expectation proves the sandwich bound. Finally, (1.1.1/1.1.13/1.4.8) bounds each arm’s excess prediction error relative to its best candidate by $2\mu(a)\Delta_{a}$, because its noise contribution is independent of the iterate. Since


1.1.1/1.1.14/1.3.46


its minimum over $\mathcal{T}^{K}$ is the sum of the armwise minima. Multiplying the armwise error bounds by $(1-\mu(a))/\mu(a)$ and summing proves (1.1.1/1.1.14/1.3.17).
