after enlarging $C_{s}$. Conditional on $A_{1:m}$, the arm-wise noises are i.i.d., and hence, on $E_{\mathrm{cnt}}$,


$$\displaystyle\operatorname{Var}(\bar{\xi}_{a}\,|\,A_{1:m})$$


Their conditional means are zero and $\sigma_{\xi,a}^{2}$, respectively. Conditional Chebyshev’s inequality and a finite union therefore give


1.1.1/1.1.15/1.1.19


The deterministic prediction bound used in 1.1.1/1.1.12/1.2.2, together with (1.1.1/1.1.15/1.1.3), also gives


1.1.1/1.1.15/1.1.20


Substitution in (1.1.1/1.1.12/1.3.12) gives


$$\displaystyle\max_{a}\sup_{t\in\mathcal{T}}|\mathcal{E}_{a,\pi}^{(t)}-\mathcal{E}_{a,\mathrm{ind}}^{(t)}|$$


for every $0<\beta<1/2$, since $d^{\beta-1/2}(\log d)^{C}\to 0$. The triangle inequality and (1.1.1/1.1.15/1.1.13) now prove the risk-criterion claim for $0<\beta<\beta_{0}$.


Finally, the proof of 1.1.1/1.1.14/1.1.8 becomes quantitative under (1.1.1/1.1.15/1.1.3). Equation (1.1.1/1.1.15/1.1.14) controls the diagonal terms. The conditional second moment in (1.1.1/1.1.14/1.1.19) is $O_{p}\{d^{-1}(\log d)^{C}\}$. Conditional Chebyshev’s inequality gives the corresponding bound for the off-diagonal averages, while the centered reward averages and arm-count deviations are $O_{p}\{d^{-1/2}(\log d)^{C}\}$. Moreover, $B_{M}=\sup_{a,t}|M_{a}^{(t)}|=O_{p}\{(\log d)^{C}\}$. In the notation of (1.1.1/1.1.14/1.1.41), these bounds give


$$\displaystyle\delta_{\mathrm{diag}}$$


for $0<\beta<\beta_{0}$. The bounds in the proof of 1.1.1/1.1.4/1.3.15 consequently yield


$$\displaystyle\sup_{\mathbf{t}\in\mathcal{T}^{K}}|\widehat{\mathcal{V}}_{\mathrm{ADM}}(\mathbf{t})-\mathcal{V}_{g_{\mathbf{t}}}(\nu)|$$


