\displaystyle\sup_{\mathbf{t}\in\mathcal{T}^{K}}|\widehat{\mathcal{V}}_{\mathrm{ADR}}(\mathbf{t})-\mathcal{V}(\nu)|# Appendix J Additional Experiments and Implementation Details## J.1 Synthetic data and network trainingUnless stated otherwise, the Gaussian experiments use independent observations with $X_{i}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I_{d})$, $A_{i}\sim\operatorname{Unif}([K])$, and $\varepsilon_{i}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,0.8^{2})$, where the contexts, actions, and noises are mutually independent. The rewards are@equation
R_{i}=\varphi_{A_{i}}^{\ast}(\beta_{A_{i}}^{\top}X_{i})+\varepsilon_{i}.The two-arm baseline uses $\varphi_{1}^{\ast}(y)=\tanh(y)$, $\varphi_{2}^{\ast}(y)=\tanh(2y)$, and the logistic target policy $\nu(1\mid x)=\sigma(2\beta_{1}^{\top}x)$, where $\sigma(u)=(1+e^{-u})^{-1}$. The signal directions are fixed, dense, and orthonormal. Other reward links and policies are specified below.For each arm, we train the network in Appendix 1.1.1/1.1.9/1.1.1 with sigmoid activation, depth $L=2$, and width $q=5$. First-layer entries are initialized independently from $\mathcal{N}(0,1/d)$ and deeper-layer entries from $\mathcal{N}(0,1/q)$, with columns $2,\ldots,q$ of the output matrix set to zero. Initial weights are fixed across datasets and shared by all procedures and folds. Every layer is updated by full-batch gradient descent on the unweighted loss $\mathcal{L}_{a}$, normalized by its arm-specific training count except in the leave-one-out diagnostic below. Candidate times are $\mathcal{T}=\{0,\ldots,30\}$. We use $\eta=2$ unless stated otherwise; the increasing-ratio experiment uses $\eta=1$ for every method.## J.2 Stopping rules and comparison estimatorsFor a full-data model, the proposed stopping rule minimizes (1.1.1/1.1.3/1.3.5) separately for each arm. We compare it with the rule that minimizes the criterion obtained by replacing the corrected predictions with $f_{W_{a}^{(t)}}(X_{i})$, and with ten-fold cross-validation. In the experiment with several target policies, we also minimize the corrected criterion with $w_{a}\equiv 1$ to isolate the effect of weighting the stopping rule for the target policy. All rules choose the smallest minimizing iteration. For cross-validation, a random partition of the complete logged sample defines the folds independently of the observations. We train each arm’s network on the complement of each fold and pool its weighted squared residuals over the held-out observations from that arm. The selected number of steps is then used to fit the model on the full arm sample from the common initialization. Consequently, every stopping rule is evaluated on the same full-data training trajectory. Its selection regret is@equation
\mathcal{R}_{\nu}^{\circ}(g_{\hat{\mathbf{t}}})-\min_{\mathbf{t}\in\mathcal{T}^{K}}\mathcal{R}_{\nu}^{\circ}(g_{\mathbf{t}}).The minimum separates over arms. The reward-noise contribution does not depend on the stopping times, so the same regret is obtained using $\mathcal{R}_{\nu}$.For risk estimation in Figures 1.1.1/3.1.1/1.1.2 and 1.1.1/3.1.1/1.1.22, we compare the criterion with $\mathcal{E}_{a,\mathrm{ind}}^{(t)}$ in (1.1.1/1.1.4/1.2.3), including the reward-noise contribution. The reported RMSE is the square root of the mean squared discrepancy, where the mean is taken over arms, candidate iterations, and datasets. Selection regret is reported on the total target-risk scale.For ten-fold cross-fitting (CF10), we randomly partition the complete logged sample into ten folds independently of the observations. Corrected stopping minimizes (1.1.1/1.1.3/1.3.5) on each arm’s complete training-complement path. For inner-validation stopping, we split the complement within each arm into $70\%$ for training and $30\%$ for selection. These partitions are independent of the contexts and rewards. We select by held-out weighted squared residuals, restart from the common initialization, and refit on the complete arm-specific complement. Both procedures use the same folds and candidate iterations, exclude the evaluation fold from fitting and selection, and average ordinary held-out DR scores with augmentation coefficient one.The inverse propensity scoring (IPS) and self-normalized inverse propensity scoring (SNIPS) estimators use the same logged sample:@equation
\widehat{\mathcal{V}}_{\mathrm{IPS}}=\frac{1}{m}\sum_{i=1}^{m}w_{A_{i}}(X_{i})R_{i},\qquad\widehat{\mathcal{V}}_{\mathrm{SNIPS}}=\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{m}w_{A_{i}}(X_{i})R_{i}}{\sum_{i=1}^{m}w_{A_{i}}(X_{i})}.For the direct comparison of the Onsager correction, we evaluate the DR estimator in (1.1.1/1.1.14/1.2.3) and ADR at identical full-data models and stopping times. We likewise compare ADM with the unadjusted empirical average of the same fitted predictions. The target in this latter comparison is $\mathcal{V}_{g_{\hat{\mathbf{t}}}}(\nu)$, evaluated separately for each fitted model.We also evaluate each arm’s selected predictor on contexts logged under other arms, without changing its training sample or stopping time:@equation
\widehat{\mathcal{V}}_{\mathrm{cross}}=\sum_{a=1}^{K}\frac{1}{m-N_{a}}\sum_{i\notin\mathcal{I}_{a}}\nu(a\mid X_{i})f_{W_{a}^{(\hat{t}_{a})}}(X_{i}).Here $\hat{t}_{a}$ is selected by the corrected criterion using only arm-$a$ data. Under context-independent logging, conditional on the action labels, the other arms’ contexts are independent of this fitted predictor; this is the off-diagonal independence argument in Lemma 1.1.1/1.1.14/1.1.8. The denominator is the actual number $m-N_{a}$ of evaluation contexts. Like DM and ADM in this comparison, cross-arm DM targets the fitted-model value $\mathcal{V}_{g_{\hat{\mathbf{t}}}}(\nu)$, rather than the true policy value.Figure 1.1.1/3.1.1/1.1.6 compares these estimators on the same 200 datasets and selected models per dimension. ADM reduces MSE relative to cross-arm DM by $50.7\%$–$57.4\%$; all five paired pointwise $95\%$ intervals favor ADM. Both estimators’ bias intervals contain zero in every setting. The gain is therefore in precision for the fitted-model value.## J.3 Vanishing-fraction holdoutsWe compare two holdout sizes fixed before running the experiment, $h=\lceil\sqrt{m}\rceil$ and $h=\lceil m^{2/3}\rceil$, on the same five Gaussian, uniform-logging settings and 200 datasets per dimension as Figure 1.1.1/3.1.1/1.1.2. Both satisfy $h\to\infty$ and $h/m\to 0$. For each dataset, one permutation independent of the observations defines nested holdouts of these sizes. Each arm’s network is trained on its complement from the common initialization. We average weighted squared residuals over that arm’s held-out observations and select the smallest minimizing iteration. The selected iteration is then evaluated on the same full-data trajectory as the other stopping rules, corresponding to full-data refitting.Table 1.1.1/3.1.1/1.1.13 reports the risk-estimation RMSE and selection regret defined in Appendix 1.1.1/1.1.16/1.3.1. Both holdouts have larger errors and regret than the corrected criterion and ten-fold CV at every dimension. For both metrics, all paired pointwise $95\%$ intervals favor the corrected criterion and CV over the holdouts; risk-estimation comparisons use MSE. Intervals use within-dataset differences, as described in Appendix 1.1.1/1.1.16/1.10.1. The two schedules thus show a finite-sample accuracy–cost trade-off: correction is more accurate, while the holdouts are cheaper (Appendix 1.1.1/1.1.16/1.12.1); their asymptotic comparison remains open.1.1.1/3.1.1/1.1.13## J.4 Target-policy weightingFigure 1.1.1/3.1.1/1.1.4 uses three arms with reward links $\varphi_{1}^{\ast}(y)=\tfrac{1}{2}+\tanh(y)+\tfrac{1}{2}\tanh^{2}(y)$, $\varphi_{2}^{\ast}(y)=-\tfrac{1}{2}+\tanh(2y)-\tfrac{1}{2}\tanh^{2}(y)$, and $\varphi_{3}^{\ast}(y)=\tfrac{1}{4}+\cos(y)$. Writing $z=(\beta_{1}^{\top}x,\beta_{2}^{\top}x,\beta_{3}^{\top}x)$, the four policies are uniform, $\operatorname{softmax}(2z)$, $\operatorname{softmax}(-2z)$, and $\operatorname{softmax}(2(z_{2},z_{3},z_{1}))$. We use $m=3d$ at $d\in\{128,256,512\}$, with 200 datasets per dimension and the network and training schedule above. Every policy uses the same logged datasets, training trajectories, and Onsager correction, so the comparison changes only the weights used for selection.Averaging within each dataset over the three nonuniform policies, weighting reduces prediction risk relative to unweighted corrected selection by $0.0315$, $0.0311$, and $0.0327$ across the three dimensions. The corresponding reductions relative to weighted ten-fold CV are $0.0281$, $0.0267$, and $0.0293$. Weighting reduces risk for $\operatorname{softmax}(2z)$ and $\operatorname{softmax}(-2z)$ at every dimension, with the larger reduction for the former. For the uniform policy, weighted and unweighted selection coincide. For the cyclically permuted policy, paired intervals for weighted versus unweighted selection contain zero at all three dimensions. The corrected weighted rule has lower mean regret than weighted CV in every policy–dimension setting; its paired interval excludes zero except for $\operatorname{softmax}(2z)$ at $d=128$.## J.5 Value estimation at a fixed sample-to-dimension ratioWe use the five $m=2d$ settings of Figure 1.1.1/3.1.1/1.1.8, with the fitting and selection protocols in Appendix 1.1.1/1.1.16/1.3.1. To compare the selected complement-trained models, we compute their target-policy prediction errors on independent test contexts, conditional on each training complement, and average across folds using held-out fold sizes, using the numerical references in Appendix 1.1.1/1.1.16/1.10.1.1.1.1/3.1.1/1.1.14For the bias–variance decomposition, let $e_{r}$ be a procedure’s policy-value error on dataset $r$, with $B=200$ datasets, and let $\bar{e}=B^{-1}\sum_{r}e_{r}$. We report empirical squared bias $\bar{e}^{2}$, variance $B^{-1}\sum_{r}(e_{r}-\bar{e})^{2}$, and MSE $B^{-1}\sum_{r}e_{r}^{2}$. The first two sum exactly to the third before rounding. The variance uses denominator $B$ and measures variation across complete value estimates with the initializations fixed. Table 1.1.1/3.1.1/1.1.15 reports these components and Student $t$ intervals for the mean squared error; the intervals quantify Monte Carlo uncertainty across datasets.1.1.1/3.1.1/1.1.15The bias intervals for ADR and both CF10 procedures contain zero at every dimension. Paired $95\%$ MSE intervals favor ADR over corrected-stopping CF10 at $d=256$ and over inner-validation CF10 at $d=256,2048$; the remaining intervals contain zero. Corrected stopping lowers CF10’s MSE point estimate at every dimension, with a paired interval excluding zero only at $d=2048$. Prediction-risk improvements need not translate proportionally into value-MSE gains: DR variance combines errors across arms, and cross-fitted estimates also include covariance between folds.ADR has lower MSE than IPS at $d=256,512,2048$ and than SNIPS at $d=2048$, with paired $95\%$ intervals below zero. The remaining intervals contain zero. These comparisons measure finite-sample MSE: with known logging, IPS is unbiased and consistent under the stated moment conditions.## J.6 Risk curves and leave-one-out refittingFigure 1.1.1/3.1.1/1.1.16 shows the two-arm risk curves at $d=512$ and $m=1024$. In later iterations, the training-residual criterion decreases while the test risk rises; the corrected criterion follows the test risk.1.1.1/3.1.1/1.1.16We also compare corrected and unadjusted predictions with networks retrained after omitting the evaluated observation. All layers are retrained from the same initialization and with the same learning rates, retaining the original arm denominator $N_{a}$ in the loss. We use $d\in\{128,256,512\}$ and $m=2d$, with 100 independent datasets per dimension and 32 rows sampled uniformly without replacement per dataset. The discrepancy is the root mean squared prediction difference over these rows and datasets.Figure 1.1.1/3.1.1/1.1.18 shows this discrepancy along training. At $t=30$, the corrected prediction’s root mean squared discrepancy decreases from $0.177$ to $0.086$ as $d$ increases from $128$ to $512$, while the unadjusted prediction’s discrepancy remains near $1.17$.1.1.1/3.1.1/1.1.18## J.7 Nonuniform loggingWe repeat the two-arm Gaussian experiment with context-independent logging probabilities $\mu(1)\in\{0.5,0.25,0.1\}$ and $\mu(2)=1-\mu(1)$. We use $d\in\{512,1024,2048\}$ and $m=2d$, with 200 independent datasets at each setting. The reward links, target policy, initial weights, network architecture, and training schedule remain as specified above, with $\eta=2$. The uniform-logging reference uses new datasets generated for this comparison.Each arm’s criterion uses $w_{a}(X)=\nu(a\mid X)/\mu(a)$. We measure error in estimating total target-policy test risk by comparing $\sum_{a=1}^{2}\mu(a)\widehat{\mathcal{E}}_{a}^{(t_{a})}$ with $\mathcal{R}_{\nu}(g_{\mathbf{t}})$, and form the corresponding comparisons for training residuals and cross-validation. The reported RMSE is the square root of the mean squared error over all $31\times 31$ pairs of stopping times and datasets. This differs from the arm-wise RMSE in Figure 1.1.1/3.1.1/1.1.2. Selection regret uses the total prediction risk $\mathcal{R}_{\nu}^{\circ}$, with every rule evaluated on the same full-data training trajectories.Figure 1.1.1/3.1.1/1.1.20 shows that the corrected criterion’s total-risk RMSE and mean selection regret decrease with dimension under each logging distribution. Its empirical mean squared risk-estimation error is smaller than cross-validation’s in all nine settings; the paired $95\%$ Monte Carlo interval for the difference excludes zero except at $\mu(1)=0.1,d=512$. The paired selection-regret intervals favor the corrected rule in every setting. For the rare arm at $\mu(1)=0.1,d=512$, however, its arm-wise risk RMSE is slightly higher than cross-validation’s, despite the lower total-risk error.The bottom row of Figure 1.1.1/3.1.1/1.1.20 compares DR and ADR at identical full-data models and stopping times selected by the corrected criterion. The correction reduces absolute empirical bias in every setting. For $\mu(1)=0.1$, the DR bias changes from $-0.143$ to $-0.193$ as dimension increases from $512$ to $2048$, while the corresponding ADR biases are $0.0004$ and $0.0025$. All nine ADR bias intervals include zero. This comparison isolates the effect of correcting the predictions used for value estimation.1.1.1/3.1.1/1.1.20## J.8 Non-Gaussian contextsWe repeat the two-arm experiment at $d\in\{256,1024,2048\}$ and $m=2d$ with independent Rademacher coordinates and with independent coordinates distributed as $\sqrt{3/5}\,T_{5}$, where $T_{5}$ has a Student $t$ distribution with five degrees of freedom. Both distributions have mean zero and variance one. At each dimension, we retain the Gaussian experiment’s reward links, target policy, initial weights, and training schedule, with independent uniform logging. Each setting uses 200 independent logged datasets.The upper panels of Figure 1.1.1/3.1.1/1.1.22 compare risk estimation under the three context laws. As dimension increases, the corrected criterion’s RMSE decreases from $0.248$ to $0.084$ for Rademacher contexts and from $0.244$ to $0.088$ for standardized $t_{5}$ contexts. At $d=2048$, the corresponding RMSEs are $0.156$ and $0.158$ for cross-validation, and $1.335$ and $1.334$ for training residuals. Table 1.1.1/3.1.1/1.1.24 compares the resulting stopping decisions on the full-data training trajectories. For both non-Gaussian laws, the corrected rule has smaller mean selection regret than ten-fold cross-validation at every dimension, with the paired $95\%$ intervals below zero.The lower panels compare DR and ADR at the same full-data models and stopping times selected by the corrected criterion, with the true policy value computed for each context law as in Appendix 1.1.1/1.1.16/1.10.1. The correction reduces absolute empirical bias in all six non-Gaussian settings. At $d=2048$, the DR and ADR biases are $-0.0191$ and $-0.0034$ for Rademacher contexts, and $-0.0159$ and $-0.0002$ for standardized $t_{5}$ contexts.1.1.1/3.1.1/1.1.221.1.1/3.1.1/1.1.24## J.9 Numerical reference values and uncertaintyReference test risks are conditional on the fitted models. For Gaussian contexts, we integrate the joint Gaussian vector of signal and policy indices and fitted first-layer projections using two independent scrambled Sobol rules with antithetic points, each containing $2^{14}$ points. For non-Gaussian contexts, we average two independent Monte Carlo estimates, each using 16,384 draws and their negatives from the specified context law. The reward-noise contribution is integrated analytically. All reference draws are independent of the logged data and are used only to assess the procedures; they do not enter fitting, selection, or the value estimators.The true policy value is computed by Gaussian integration, a finite sum over binomial sign counts for Rademacher contexts, or numerical inversion of the signal sums’ characteristic functions for standardized $t_{5}$ contexts. Errors of DM, ADM, and cross-arm DM are measured against the average of the two independent numerical references for each fitted model’s value.For normalized value MSE, we compute $\sigma_{\mathrm{eff}}^{2}=\operatorname{Var}\{\psi_{g^{\ast}}(X,A,R)\}$ by Gaussian integration and use the true-reward score average $\widehat{\mathcal{V}}_{\mathrm{or}}=m^{-1}\sum_{i}\psi_{g^{\ast}}(Q_{i})$ as a control variate. Specifically, we average@equation
1+\frac{m}{\sigma_{\mathrm{eff}}^{2}}\left\{(\widehat{\mathcal{V}}-\mathcal{V}(\nu))^{2}-(\widehat{\mathcal{V}}_{\mathrm{or}}-\mathcal{V}(\nu))^{2}\right\}over datasets; the control squared error has mean $\sigma_{\mathrm{eff}}^{2}/m$.All pointwise $95\%$ intervals use Student $t$ quantiles across independent logged datasets. They describe Monte Carlo uncertainty in performance, rather than uncertainty in policy value from a single dataset. RMSE intervals take square roots of the MSE limits after truncating the lower limit at zero. Comparisons use within-dataset differences; in the target-policy experiment, averages over nonuniform policies are also formed within each dataset before computing intervals.## J.10 Value estimation as sample size outgrows dimensionWe compare ADR, DR with ten-fold cross-fitting (CF10), inverse propensity scoring (IPS), and self-normalized IPS (SNIPS) as the number of observations increases relative to the context dimension. We use the two-arm model above and train every reward network with $\eta=1$ over candidate iterations $\{0,\ldots,30\}$. Both corrected-stopping and inner-validation CF10 use the protocols in Appendix 1.1.1/1.1.16/1.3.1; each evaluation fold is excluded from fitting and selection. We first fix $d=256$ and increase $m\in\{512,2048,8192,32768\}$. We also increase both dimension and sample-to-dimension ratio along $(d,m)\in\{(256,2048),(512,8192),(1024,32768)\}$. Each setting uses 200 independent datasets, except those with $m=32768$, which use 100.We report normalized mean squared error, $m\mathbb{E}[(\widehat{\mathcal{V}}-\mathcal{V}(\nu))^{2}]/\sigma_{\mathrm{eff}}^{2}$, where $\sigma_{\mathrm{eff}}^{2}=\operatorname{Var}\{\psi_{g^{\ast}}(X,A,R)\}$. The normalization $\sigma_{\mathrm{eff}}^{2}/m$ is the exact MSE of DR using the true reward function, whose normalized MSE is therefore one for every $(d,m)$. At a fixed data law, $\sigma_{\mathrm{eff}}^{2}$ is also the classical semiparametric variance bound for known logging and otherwise unrestricted context and reward distributions. We use it here as an oracle reference. We estimate normalized MSE using the control-variate calculation in Appendix 1.1.1/1.1.16/1.10.1.Figure 1.1.1/3.1.1/1.1.10 shows ADR and both CF10 procedures approaching normalized MSE one as sample size outgrows dimension. At $(d,m)=(1024,32768)$, ADR and corrected-stopping CF10 give $1.000$ and $1.013$, respectively. At both endpoints with $m=32768$, paired ADR-minus-corrected-CF10 intervals contain zero, while ADR’s MSE is lower than IPS and SNIPS with paired intervals below zero.## J.11 Computational costADR requires one full-data training trajectory per arm. Its response recursion adds $O(N_{a}q^{3}T^{3})$ operations and $O(N_{a}T^{2}q^{2})$ storage (Appendix 1.1.1/1.1.8/1.1.1). Corrected-stopping CF10 repeats this calculation on ten training complements. Table 1.1.1/3.1.1/1.1.25 shows that ADR is $5.5$–$8.9$ times faster in the three benchmark settings. CV and inner-validation CF10 are faster in the smallest setting, while ADR is faster in the larger two.Both tables report median wall time over five shared datasets per setting, using the two-arm Gaussian model and training settings above, with $\eta=2$ when $m=2d$ and $\eta=1$ at $m=32768$. Each measurement includes training, selection, refitting where required, and final value estimation; common data generation and reference integration are excluded. Runs use one numerical thread, with a separate warm-up excluded. The studies were conducted on different CPUs, so comparisons use the controls within each table.1.1.1/3.1.1/1.1.25The single holdouts in Table 1.1.1/3.1.1/1.1.26 are faster than ADR. Together with Appendix 1.1.1/1.1.16/1.4.1, this identifies the trade-off: the correction improves risk estimation and stopping selection, while a single holdout costs less. The CV and holdout timings include full-data refitting and raw DR scoring; these procedures compare selection costs, since refitting alone does not correct same-sample DR bias.1.1.1/3.1.1/1.1.26author: S. Arlot and A. Celisse
